Friday's sidecar (the Python browser agent, the Laya router and the optional speech helpers in Sidecar/) is a copy
of the author's Jarvis backend, which incorporates and builds on the following open-source work.

jev-ultrafast — https://github.com/browser-use/jev-ultrafast  (MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Browser Use)
  The observed-action architecture: indexed elements from a DOM snapshot, node identity, freshness markers (page key /
  guard / marker), visibility and occlusion checks, and the "choose, don't generate" action space. Derived code:
  Sidecar/jarvis/page.js (from snapshot.js) and the executor semantics in Sidecar/jarvis/browser.py and
  Sidecar/jarvis/goal_runner.py.

laya-ultrafast — https://github.com/ipenywis/laya-ultrafast  (MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Browser Use; port of jev-ultrafast)
  The Laya decision policy that composes narrow typed questions with site-independent rules. Derived code:
  Sidecar/jarvis/policy.py (from laya.py) and its tests (Sidecar/tests/test_policy.py, from tests/test_laya.py), the
  sub-goal planning prompt in Sidecar/jarvis/prompts.py, and the Forma fixture Sidecar/tests/fixtures/site/forma.html.

browser-harness — https://github.com/browser-use/browser-harness  (MIT, Browser Use)
  The approach to attaching to the user's Chrome over the DevTools Protocol, including the Chrome 144+ "Allow remote
  debugging" flow and DevToolsActivePort discovery (Sidecar/jarvis/chrome.py, Sidecar/jarvis/cdp.py). Friday talks CDP
  directly from the sidecar process and does not include browser-harness code or its telemetry.

laya-mlx — https://github.com/mizorewww/laya-mlx  (Apache-2.0)
  The MLX runtime used to run Laya locally; installed as a dependency.

Laya — https://github.com/NandhaKishorM/laya  (Convai Innovations; Apache-2.0)
  The typed-decision model. The sidecar downloads the converted checkpoint aac6fef/laya-typed-decisions-mlx from
  Hugging Face on first use.

Other dependencies keep their own licenses: FastAPI/Starlette/uvicorn (BSD), httpx (BSD), websockets (BSD),
huggingface-hub (Apache-2.0), keyring (MIT), pydantic (MIT), MLX (MIT). Friday's own sidecar code is MIT (see the
repository's LICENSE).

